      "text": "To solve the problem of finding the distance between the points \\((-3, -4)\\) and \\((4, -5)\\), we can use the distance formula. The distance formula for two points \\((x_1, y_1)\\) and \\((x_2, y_2)\\) in a coordinate plane is given by:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{(x_2 - x_1)^2 + (y_2 - y_1)^2}\n\\]\n\nHere, the coordinates of the first point are \\((x_1, y_1) = (-3, -4)\\) and the coordinates of the second point are \\((x_2, y_2) = (4, -5)\\). Plugging these coordinates into the distance formula, we get:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{(4 - (-3))^2 + (-5 - (-4))^2}\n\\]\n\nSimplify the expressions inside the parentheses:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{(4 + 3)^2 + (-5 + 4)^2} = \\sqrt{7^2 + (-1)^2}\n\\]\n\nCalculate the squares:\n\n\\[\nd = \\sqrt{49 + 1} = \\sqrt{50}\n\\]\n\nSimplify \\(\\sqrt{50}\\):\n\n\\[\n\\sqrt{50} = \\sqrt{25 \\cdot 2} = \\sqrt{25} \\cdot \\sqrt{2} = 5\\sqrt{2}\n\\]\n\nTherefore, the distance between the points \\((-3, -4)\\) and \\((4, -5)\\) is \\(\\boxed{5\\sqrt{2}}\\).",
